---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Cite from '../components/content/Cite.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/content/Placeholder.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import { engagements, principles, stakeholders } from '../data/human-practices';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'approach', title: 'How we work with people' },
  { id: 'stakeholders', title: 'Who we need to hear from' },
  { id: 'engagements', title: 'What we heard, and what changed' },
  { id: 'context', title: 'Designing for Kazakhstan' },
  { id: 'principles', title: 'Principles we hold ourselves to' },
];

const refs = ['avizova2026', 'akhmedullin2024', 'wilson1968'];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="human-practices"
  toc={toc}
  refs={refs}
  brief={[
    'A screening test is a health-system decision: who is tested, what a result means, and what happens next.',
    'We list who we need to hear from and what we will ask them, and we record only conversations that have happened.',
    'Affordability, portability and follow-up capacity shape the design as much as the biology does.',
  ]}
>
  <Section id="approach" title="How we work with people">
    <p>
      Human practices is not a survey at the end of a project. The questions we ask other people change what we build,
      so we treat each conversation as an input to the design and record what it changed.
    </p>
    <ol class="loop">
      <li><span>Ask</span>People who would use, prescribe or regulate a breath test.</li>
      <li><span>Understand</span>What constrains them: cost, time, trust, infrastructure, law.</li>
      <li><span>Change</span>The design, the claims, or the plan, and write down which.</li>
      <li><span>Check back</span>Return with the change and ask whether it actually helps.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section id="stakeholders" title="Who we need to hear from">
    <p>
      These are the groups whose answers would change the project, with the questions we intend to put to them.
      Publishing the questions before the conversations keeps us honest about what we learned afterwards.
    </p>

    <div class="stakeholders">
      {
        stakeholders.map((group) => (
          <section class="stakeholder" aria-labelledby={`sh-${group.id}`}>
            <h3 id={`sh-${group.id}`}>{group.name}</h3>
            <p class="stakeholder__who">{group.who}</p>
            <p class="stakeholder__why">{group.whyThem}</p>
            <h4>What we ask</h4>
            <ul>
              {group.whatWeAsk.map((question) => (
                <li>{question}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="engagements" title="What we heard, and what changed">
    {
      engagements.length === 0 ? (
        <>
          <p>
            No engagements have taken place yet. When they do, each one will be recorded here with the date, who we
            spoke to and, most importantly, what we changed as a result.
          </p>
          <Placeholder title="Engagement log" where="src/data/human-practices.ts">
            <ul>
              <li>Date, who was involved and in what capacity.</li>
              <li>What they told us, including anything that contradicted our assumptions.</li>
              <li>The specific change we made to the design, claims or plan.</li>
            </ul>
          </Placeholder>
        </>
      ) : (
        <Table label="Engagement log">
          <table class="data">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Date</th>
                <th scope="col">Who</th>
                <th scope="col">What we discussed</th>
                <th scope="col">What we changed</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {engagements.map((e) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{e.date}</th>
                  <td>{e.who}</td>
                  <td>{e.what}</td>
                  <td>{e.changed}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </Table>
      )
    }
  </Section>

  <Section id="context" title="Designing for Kazakhstan">
    <p>
      The project starts from a local problem. Lung cancers were the largest share of cancer deaths recorded nationally
      between 2014 and 2022,<Cite id="akhmedullin2024" /> and patients in the 2019–2023 national cohort were usually
      recorded at an advanced stage.<Cite id="avizova2026" /> A test that could be produced and run locally, without a
      scanner, is worth investigating for that reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whether it would help depends on facts we do not have yet: where screening capacity sits today, how far people
      travel for a scan, and what a clinic can afford to run. Those are questions for the people in the section above,
      and Wilson and Jungner's principle that facilities must exist for diagnosis and treatment applies before any of
      this is useful.<Cite id="wilson1968" />
    </p>

    <Placeholder title="Kazakhstan healthcare context, with sources" where="src/pages/human-practices.astro">
      <ul>
        <li>How lung cancer screening and referral work today, cited to official sources.</li>
        <li>Distribution of CT capacity and oncology services by region.</li>
        <li>What a primary care clinic could realistically afford per test.</li>
        <li>Notes from conversations with the groups listed above.</li>
      </ul>
    </Placeholder>

    <Note kind="hypothesis" title="Design targets, to be checked with the people who would use it">
      <p>
        We are designing towards a test that can be produced with standard laboratory equipment, read with simple
        optics, and run by trained staff without a specialist laboratory. Each of those targets needs to be confirmed
        with clinicians and manufacturers before it means anything.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="principles" title="Principles we hold ourselves to">
    <ol class="principles">
      {
        principles.map((principle) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{principle.title}</h3>
            <p>{principle.detail}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    <p>
      The safety, ethics and validation consequences of these principles are set out in{' '}
      <a href={href('safety-and-security')}>Safety and ethics</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>

<style>
  .loop {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    counter-reset: loop;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .loop {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: var(--sp-4);
    }
  }

  .loop li {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    padding: var(--sp-4);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
  }

  .loop span {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .stakeholders {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-4);
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .stakeholders {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .stakeholder:first-child {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }

  .stakeholder {
    padding: var(--sp-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-figure);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .stakeholder h3 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .stakeholder__who {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .stakeholder__why {
    margin-top: var(--sp-3);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  .stakeholder h4 {
    margin-top: var(--sp-4);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .stakeholder ul {
    margin: var(--sp-2) 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.05em;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .stakeholder li + li {
    margin-top: var(--sp-1);
  }

  .principles {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .principles li {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    padding-block: var(--sp-5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .principles li {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
      gap: var(--sp-6);
    }
  }

  .principles h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .principles p {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
</style>
